[{:db/id 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0237c8ad-eb27-521a-9b96-cd671ad62b96" "question-10a98313-2524-5ce8-a6f0-176526143e93"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198096 :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x):$ $x^2+2x=3$\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\mathbb{Z}.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ x \\in U \\: | \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U = \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nWe need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x^2+2x=3$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-3\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $x=1\\in\\mathbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T = \\{-3,1\\}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-86866e7c-73c9-5409-8743-b71ad4d3cbe4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198097 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $x \\geq y$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{p}{q}$ is a rational number.\n\nIII. $a$ is a positive number." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set,* and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(p,q)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $a$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-713f4e9c-4d96-5c9b-be62-26529dd25eb1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198098 :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, | \\, q$, \n\nwhere the universal set is $\\big\\{ (p,q) \\: | \\: p = 1,2,6, \\: q = 3,6,9 \\big\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: | \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\{1,2,6\\}\\times \\{3,6,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{(1,3),(1,6),(1,9),(2,3),(2,6),(2,9),(6,3),(6,6),(6,9)\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $(p,q) \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, | \\, q$\n\nThe following pairs give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $(1,3)$ since $1$ divides $3,$\n\n- $(1,6)$ since $1$ divides $6,$\n\n- $(1,9)$ since $1$ divides $9,$\n\n- $(2,6)$ since $2$ divides $6,$\n\n- $(6,6)$ since $6$ divides $6.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (1,3), (1,6), (1,9), (2,6), (6,6) \\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0237c8ad-eb27-521a-9b96-cd671ad62b96" :question/id #uuid "0237c8ad-eb27-521a-9b96-cd671ad62b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-175372" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}<9$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ ${x}^{2}<9$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides of the inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & <\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |x| & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the definition of absolute value, we must have\n\n$$\n-3\\text{ }<x<3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T=(-3,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5aa0841-040f-5aec-85ca-fd3f64c07a51"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5aa0841-040f-5aec-85ca-fd3f64c07a51" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5aa0841-040f-5aec-85ca-fd3f64c07a51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5d21df02-6b8d-5e86-a2f9-f452aee05936" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d21df02-6b8d-5e86-a2f9-f452aee05936" "answer-61c53b8b-e43b-5a05-9fb3-abadd0684739" "answer-4ff057a1-4c2b-565a-83cd-56aa95d66144" "answer-f99f6fd4-23c4-5261-9b7b-39d01859eba3" "answer-3cde1ebc-e432-58be-aec4-61a0a888f799"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d21df02-6b8d-5e86-a2f9-f452aee05936" :answer/id #uuid "5d21df02-6b8d-5e86-a2f9-f452aee05936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61c53b8b-e43b-5a05-9fb3-abadd0684739" :answer/id #uuid "61c53b8b-e43b-5a05-9fb3-abadd0684739" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ff057a1-4c2b-565a-83cd-56aa95d66144" :answer/id #uuid "4ff057a1-4c2b-565a-83cd-56aa95d66144" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f99f6fd4-23c4-5261-9b7b-39d01859eba3" :answer/id #uuid "f99f6fd4-23c4-5261-9b7b-39d01859eba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cde1ebc-e432-58be-aec4-61a0a888f799" :answer/id #uuid "3cde1ebc-e432-58be-aec4-61a0a888f799" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-10a98313-2524-5ce8-a6f0-176526143e93" :question/id #uuid "10a98313-2524-5ce8-a6f0-176526143e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-175361" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x):$ $5x+2>22,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $R.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{x\\in U\\,|\\,P(x)\\}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $x\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $5x+2>22$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U=R.$\n\nNow, we need to solve the above inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2>22 \\\\ 5x>20 \\\\ x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT=(4,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-97aeada7-12fc-5a6f-b3b8-8465455bada0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97aeada7-12fc-5a6f-b3b8-8465455bada0" :answer-field/id #uuid "97aeada7-12fc-5a6f-b3b8-8465455bada0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d143d075-6812-5d2c-bd24-7bc7894f07b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4423da0-b4c9-5f1f-83a8-6ee54f9efc80" "answer-f4e815df-53f8-5522-8977-b3d95d5b221e" "answer-58b7a612-07c6-5679-8d42-d625deb5e970" "answer-d143d075-6812-5d2c-bd24-7bc7894f07b7" "answer-e210c4b1-8a09-558c-89b2-8405b5a063c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4423da0-b4c9-5f1f-83a8-6ee54f9efc80" :answer/id #uuid "d4423da0-b4c9-5f1f-83a8-6ee54f9efc80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4e815df-53f8-5522-8977-b3d95d5b221e" :answer/id #uuid "f4e815df-53f8-5522-8977-b3d95d5b221e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58b7a612-07c6-5679-8d42-d625deb5e970" :answer/id #uuid "58b7a612-07c6-5679-8d42-d625deb5e970" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d143d075-6812-5d2c-bd24-7bc7894f07b7" :answer/id #uuid "d143d075-6812-5d2c-bd24-7bc7894f07b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e210c4b1-8a09-558c-89b2-8405b5a063c1" :answer/id #uuid "e210c4b1-8a09-558c-89b2-8405b5a063c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198721 :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $x<y$\n\nII. $n$ is a perfect square.\n\nIII. Are $n$ and $m$ coprime?" :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $n$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(n,m)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186194952 :answer-field/correct 17592186194956 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-86866e7c-73c9-5409-8743-b71ad4d3cbe4" :question/id #uuid "86866e7c-73c9-5409-8743-b71ad4d3cbe4" :question/math-academy-id "q-175366" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $P(y):$ ${y}^{2}-1=8$\n\nII. $Q(a,b):$ ${a}^{2}+{b}^{2}$\n\nIII. $R(x,y):$ $\\frac{x}{y}\\in N\\cap Q$" :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set*, and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $y$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is *not* a predicate. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(a,b)$ into the sentence, we do not get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-36434b4b-dfd9-5274-923b-5cb5af1c5fbf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-36434b4b-dfd9-5274-923b-5cb5af1c5fbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "36434b4b-dfd9-5274-923b-5cb5af1c5fbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89c42d97-1ffe-532c-88ad-c4354d4cacc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89c42d97-1ffe-532c-88ad-c4354d4cacc9" "answer-dd2f7abc-c440-5908-9420-fb38212f6415" "answer-3663dea6-8f8d-54bc-864f-6cdf19f19c88" "answer-8aae07b3-7050-585d-9ab2-29e7b26a079e" "answer-9267effc-1c42-535d-b1dc-8a9d238e9d00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89c42d97-1ffe-532c-88ad-c4354d4cacc9" :answer/id #uuid "89c42d97-1ffe-532c-88ad-c4354d4cacc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd2f7abc-c440-5908-9420-fb38212f6415" :answer/id #uuid "dd2f7abc-c440-5908-9420-fb38212f6415" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3663dea6-8f8d-54bc-864f-6cdf19f19c88" :answer/id #uuid "3663dea6-8f8d-54bc-864f-6cdf19f19c88" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aae07b3-7050-585d-9ab2-29e7b26a079e" :answer/id #uuid "8aae07b3-7050-585d-9ab2-29e7b26a079e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9267effc-1c42-535d-b1dc-8a9d238e9d00" :answer/id #uuid "9267effc-1c42-535d-b1dc-8a9d238e9d00" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-713f4e9c-4d96-5c9b-be62-26529dd25eb1" :question/id #uuid "713f4e9c-4d96-5c9b-be62-26529dd25eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-175390" :question/problem "Consider the following predicate:\n\n$\\,$ $P(x,y):$ $x+y\\le 0,$\n\nwhere the universal set is ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following shaded regions represents the truth set of the predicate?" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U={U}_{1}\\times {U}_{2}\\times ⋯\\times {U}_{n}$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT=\\{({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\in U\\,|\\,P({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U={R}^{2}.$\n\nWe need to find $(x,y)\\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\,$ $x+y\\le 0$\n\nNow, we convert the inequality into the slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's sketch the truth set $T$ of $P(x,y):$\n\n![](images/1e/1e8f03a8b229a8b2e46af33c700dec57a8abed693a58c75b6312e2ae5a6856c1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd267c3d-501f-5503-9b5e-c946c034c0d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd267c3d-501f-5503-9b5e-c946c034c0d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd267c3d-501f-5503-9b5e-c946c034c0d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a94059a-c75b-54d5-86b6-d7a3d7e98d53" :answer-field/choices ["answer-722f7e6a-002b-544e-97b2-40986c6598f4" "answer-13edba04-7016-55b3-998a-0c1a02b2d307" "answer-1a94059a-c75b-54d5-86b6-d7a3d7e98d53" "answer-d49d4e89-b150-588d-b862-2ff8d4d1e8e1" "answer-d882ad0a-e078-5ea7-908f-611bb3476cea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-722f7e6a-002b-544e-97b2-40986c6598f4" :answer/id #uuid "722f7e6a-002b-544e-97b2-40986c6598f4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/1d/1de0fbb5b6a44423038b980a1e57e1486f02335e1a317ee143f6a6dfa76e7911.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13edba04-7016-55b3-998a-0c1a02b2d307" :answer/id #uuid "13edba04-7016-55b3-998a-0c1a02b2d307" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/50/500233e26320d4faa984864948646c0a36b6a0672a3b5b5871b234f49656ac56.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a94059a-c75b-54d5-86b6-d7a3d7e98d53" :answer/id #uuid "1a94059a-c75b-54d5-86b6-d7a3d7e98d53" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8c/8c80576fe81dd0978e5e8a4ecd937c5e71e60297dcc94ccec3751cc3bea1b11d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d49d4e89-b150-588d-b862-2ff8d4d1e8e1" :answer/id #uuid "d49d4e89-b150-588d-b862-2ff8d4d1e8e1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a7/a7fe5877fd1ec762f6d9ebdeceb8b6739d204081430db291ad3e1284a63d12eb.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d882ad0a-e078-5ea7-908f-611bb3476cea" :answer/id #uuid "d882ad0a-e078-5ea7-908f-611bb3476cea" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/c1/c139a1b3b413a1cbf60789de2cdc831055b1407c101e4fa63992a814f44cc78d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199541 :tutorial/content "Recall that a *predicate* of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some universal set and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either true or false.\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ x \\in U \\: | \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $D(x):$ $x$ is divisible by $2,$\n\nwhere the universal set is $U = \\big\\{ 1,2, \\ldots, 6 \\big\\}.$\n\nTo find the truth set $T,$ we need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x$ is divisible by $2.$\n\nLet's check all the elements in our universal set $U$ to determine which of them are elements of the truth set $T{:}$\n\n- $x=1\\notin T$ since $1$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=2\\in T$ since $2$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=3\\notin T$ since $3$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=4\\in T$ since $4$ is divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=5\\notin T$ since $5$ is *not* divisible by $2$\n\n- $x=6\\in T$ since $6$ is divisible by $2$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ 2,4,6 \\big\\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199540 :tutorial/content "A predicate of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains these variables with the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false. \n\nWe can denote such a predicate using the following notation:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nIf $U_1$ is the universal set for $x_1,U_2$ is the universal set for $x_2,\\ldots,$ etc, then the universal set $U$ for our predicate is given by the Cartesian product\n\n$$\nU = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n.\n$$\n\nFor example, the sentence\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x,y): x+y \\lt 2,$ $\\quad$ $x,y \\in \\mathbb{R}$\n\nis a predicate of two variables, $x$ and $y.$ In this case, the universal set is\n\n$$\nU = \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} = \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nThe **truth set** $T$ of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples $(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n) \\in U$ such that substituting these tuples into the predicate gives a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: | \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example, to determine the truth set of the predicate $P(x,y)$ defined above, we need to find $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x+y \\lt 2$\n\nTo find the truth set, we first find an explicit condition on $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & <2 \\\\ y & <2-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set contains all tuples $(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2$ that satisfy this inequality. We can write the truth set $T$ of our predicate as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: y < 2-x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $T$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we can represent it as a region in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebd1253b4980e2be41247128a426412adafe34a64fdc38d2d7141cb20d62354.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198513 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
